{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789810318,"nonce":"GFrbKCzIMaL8/t/I+xcwzp+BVAA=","title":"Beat LuaJIT on every site benchmark","message":"The site's benchmark page compares the default build (Cranelift baseline, no top tier) against LuaJIT on the same runner. From the 2026-09-19 run on ubuntu x86_64 (best of 10):\n\n| bench | wrenlift | luajit | gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| fib | 0.0146 | 0.0112 | 1.3x |\n| method_call | 0.0262 | 0.0068 | 3.9x |\n| binary_trees | 0.204 | 1.053 | ahead |\n| delta_blue | 0.081 | no port | |\n| nbody (500k steps) | 0.469 | 0.095 | 4.9x |\n| mandelbrot (350x200x200) | 0.041 | 0.025 | 1.6x |\n\nOn this Mac, Ash's mirrors (bench/ash) take 7.6 s (nbody, 10M steps) and 1.05 s (mandelbrot) on the default build; 4.6 s / 1.06 s with WLIFT_TIER1=cranelift; 1.1 s / 0.89 s with the llvm feature. Ash runs them in 0.6-0.7 s and 0.55 s. Both need to come under 1 s.\n\nWhere the time goes in nbody (sampled, WLIFT_JIT_CODE_DIR disassembly):\n- Every getter and setter on Body is a `wren_call_0/1` helper call with the trivial-accessor fast path in the runtime: the Cranelift tiers only inline an accessor for IC kind 5, and the optimised MIR keeps them as `call` because `known_call_sites` skips bodies without arithmetic. The LLVM lowering splices them behind a class guard from `inline_bodies`, which is where its 4x over Cranelift comes from.\n- `_bodies[i]` / `_bodies[j]` are `wren_subscript_get` helper calls in both Cranelift tiers, 150M of them; the LLVM lowering has `list_element` inline.\n- With `WLIFT_INLINE_ACCESSORS=1` the MIR inliner splices accessors at the top tier. The result is slower on both backends (Cranelift 7.6 s, LLVM 4.4 s): each site splits its continuation and versions the loop, so the fast copy is a chain of blocks with 20-30 parameters, and Cranelift spills them all (a 6 KB frame, 1400 loads and 980 stores in 7000 instructions). The shape has to change before that switch can be on: inline into the block in place with a guard that exits (deopt) instead of versioning, and let the type specialiser see field loads as Num from the class's field-kind profile instead of a per-site result guard.\n- The optimised-tier MIR still boxes between every op the specialiser could not prove; fmul/fadd appear 8 times where the loop does 30 flops per pair.\n\nOrder of work:\n1. Accessor inlining in MIR without loop versioning (guard-and-exit), so both backends see field loads; field-kind profile drives Num speculation on those loads.\n2. Inline list subscript get/set on a known List receiver in the Cranelift lowering (LLVM has it).\n3. Decide the shipped top tier: the release and bench builds are Cranelift-only; Ash ships LLVM. With LLVM promoted per function, nbody is already 1.1 s.\n4. method_call and fib: call overhead through the interpreter-to-native seam and `wren_call_N`; direct slot calls exist behind `direct_calls_enabled`.\n5. Re-run the site bench after each and record here.","files":null}]}